== 1. Install ===============================================================================

On this bench (fastest; paths relative to your project directory):

  uv pip install --python .venv/bin/python -e ../uwb-kit        # into YOUR venv (deps: aiohttp, numpy, pyserial; wheels only)
  # or use the kit's own ready-made venv without installing anything:
  ../uwb-kit/.venv/bin/python -m uwbkit --version

Anywhere else (Windows laptop, UNO Q, a clean clone), Python >= 3.10:

  python -m venv .venv
  .venv/bin/pip install ./uwb-kit              # Windows: .venv\Scripts\pip install .\uwb-kit
  .venv/bin/python -m uwbkit --version         # Windows: .venv\Scripts\python -m uwbkit --version

Vendor it when your project is done (copies, so your repo stands alone):

  ../uwb-kit/.venv/bin/python -m uwbkit vendor vendor/uwbkit                 # whole kit (hub + sim + solver + clients)
  ../uwb-kit/.venv/bin/python -m uwbkit vendor vendor/uwbkit --client-only   # just client.py + uwbkit-client.js
  # then:  pip install ./vendor/uwbkit     or     PYTHONPATH=vendor/uwbkit python -m uwbkit hub ...

== 2. Run your own hub (own port = your project port + 100, own scenario file) ==============

  python -m uwbkit hub --source sim --scenario scenarios/my-scenario.json --port 1920
  python -m uwbkit hub --source sim --scenario two-tags --port 1920          # a built-in scenario by name
  python -m uwbkit hub --source sim --profile mesh5 --port 1920              # first built-in with that profile
  python -m uwbkit hub --source replay:session.ndjson --loop --port 1920     # replay a recording
  python -m uwbkit hub --source serial:auto                                  # real boards on USB
  python -m uwbkit hub --source udp:0.0.0.0:1801                             # real boards over Wi-Fi

  (`python` = the interpreter where uwbkit is installed, e.g. .venv/bin/python or ../uwb-kit/.venv/bin/python;
   the console script `uwbkit hub ...` is equivalent. Other flags: --host 127.0.0.1[,more] --profile 4A1T
   --seed N --speed 2 --record file.ndjson --record-dir DIR --tag-z 1.0 --dims 3. `uwbkit hub --help` lists all.)

  Point your code at it with the URL argument or the environment variable both clients honour:
      UWBKIT_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:1920/stream

  Scenario files follow the schema in INTERFACE.md exactly. Built-in scenarios (`python -m uwbkit scenarios`):
      office-walk (default)  clinic-hallway  two-tags  mesh-dance  static-cal  nlos-stress
      figure-eight  tabletop  cube-3d
  They live in uwb-kit/uwbkit/sim/scenarios/*.json: copy one as a starting point for your own.
  A bad scenario file fails with a readable message that names the offending key.

  Launching the hub from your demo launcher (cross-platform, no shell tricks):
      import subprocess, sys
      hub = subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, "-m", "uwbkit", "hub", "--source", "sim",
                              "--scenario", "scenarios/my-scenario.json", "--port", "1920"])

== 3. Python client (stdlib only, one file: uwb-kit/uwbkit/client.py) ========================

  from uwbkit.client import Stream, Kit
  for msg in Stream("ws://127.0.0.1:1800/stream"):          # reconnects automatically; url=None -> $UWBKIT_URL
      if msg["type"] == "hello": print("SIM" if msg["simulated"] else "REAL", msg["nodes"])
      if msg["type"] == "pos":   print(msg["id"], msg["x"], msg["y"], msg["q"], msg["src"])
      if msg["type"] == "event": print(msg["id"], msg["name"], msg["data"])

  asyncio:      async for msg in AsyncStream(url): ...
  filter:       Stream(url, types={"pos", "event"})          # .hello / .anchors / .simulated stay available
  SSE instead:  Stream("http://127.0.0.1:1800/events")
  REST:         kit = Kit("http://127.0.0.1:1920"); kit.state(); kit.teleport("T1", 1.0, 2.0);
                kit.set_scenario("two-tags"); kit.sim_control("pause"); kit.record("start", "run1.ndjson");
                kit.truth()   # sim ground truth: for metrics in results/ only, never for a demo UI

== 4. JavaScript client (one file, no dependencies: uwb-kit/uwbkit/static/uwbkit-client.js) ==

  <script src="http://localhost:1800/uwbkit-client.js"></script>     <!-- every hub serves it; or vendor the file -->
  <script>
    const kit = new UwbKit("ws://localhost:1800/stream");            // reconnects automatically
    kit.simBadge();                                                   // shows a SIM badge while hello.simulated is true
    kit.on("pos", p => draw(p.id, p.x, p.y, p.q));
    kit.on("event", e => console.log(e.id, e.name, e.data));
  </script>

  Node >= 22:   const UwbKit = require("./vendor/uwbkit/uwbkit-client.js");   // same API
  State cache:  kit.hello, kit.anchors, kit.pos["T1"], kit.ranges["T1-A1"], kit.status, kit.simulated
  REST:         await kit.state(); kit.teleport("T1", 1, 2); kit.setScenario("two-tags"); kit.simControl("pause");
                kit.record("start", "run1.ndjson"); kit.post("/sim/control", {cmd: "speed", value: 2})
  Phone:        adb reverse tcp:1920 tcp:1920   (the phone then opens http://localhost:1920/...)

== 5. What the stream gives you (details: INTERFACE.md) ======================================

  hello (first), anchors (second), then range (per link, ~10 Hz), pos (per tag; per node in mesh5),
  event (motion_start, motion_stop, proximity, button, node_online, node_offline), status (1 Hz).
  hello may be sent AGAIN on a live connection when the configuration changes (scenario switch, a new
  board appears): replace your node list when you see it.
  Simulator noise model (also in hello.sim.noise): Gaussian range noise, positive NLOS bias + extra noise
  + attenuation per wall crossed, positive-skewed outliers, dropouts. Numbers from it are SIMULATED.

